+++ b/docs/memory/task/task-003-fix-json-patch-rfc-6902-conformance-bugs.md
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+---
+id: task-003-fix-json-patch-rfc-6902-conformance-bugs
+type: task
+title: "Fix JSON Patch RFC 6902 conformance bugs"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` report surprising results: some patches they consider invalid are accepted, some
+edge cases give results they did not expect, and a patch that fails does not always behave the way they
+read the standard. Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour conform to
+RFC 6902 in every case, errors included.
+
+## What was done
+
+Reviewed `validateOperation`, `addAt`, `removeAt`, `replaceAt`, and `applyOperation` in `src/index.ts`
+against RFC 6902 and dl-002, and probed them with the RFC appendix A examples plus additional edge
+cases (root-path operations, `__proto__`-named members, shared/mutated `value` references, move
+self-targets and array-append targets, malformed operations). Found and fixed two conformance bugs;
+everything else probed (validation ordering, array index rules, `move`'s remove-then-add sequencing,
+`test`'s deep-equality, atomicity on failure) already matched the RFC and dl-002.
+
+- **`add` silently dropped a member named `"__proto__"`.** `addAt`'s object branch wrote new members
+  with plain property assignment (`parent[lastToken] = value`). For a brand-new member named
+  `"__proto__"`, that expression does not create an own property at all: it invokes
+  `Object.prototype`'s `__proto__` accessor, which either repoints the object's own prototype (when
+  `value` is itself an object or `null`) or is silently ignored (otherwise). Either way, `add` reported
+  success but the member was never actually added — `JSON.stringify` of the result showed `{}`. Fixed
+  by creating the member with `Object.defineProperty(parent, lastToken, { value, writable: true,
+  enumerable: true, configurable: true })`, which always creates a real own data property regardless of
+  its name. (`replace`'s existing assignment was already safe, since `replace` only ever touches a
+  member that `step` has already confirmed exists as an own property, so the own-property lookup always
+  shadows the prototype accessor before any assignment happens.)
+- **`add` and `replace` aliased the operation's `value` into the result instead of cloning it.** dl-002
+  already decided that "every operation... deep-clones the value it inserts," specifically to prevent a
+  value aliasing two locations, but only `copy` actually did so (`structuredClone(value)` before calling
+  `addAt`). `add` and `replace` passed `operation.value` straight through. Passing the same literal
+  `value` to two `add` operations in one patch, or mutating the `value` object after calling
+  `applyPatch`, visibly changed the already-returned result — a direct violation of the "`document` is
+  never mutated" / no-aliasing guarantee the README and dl-002 both promise, extended here to the
+  patch's own input values. Fixed by cloning with `structuredClone(operation.value)` at the two call
+  sites in `applyOperation` for `add` and `replace`. `move` needed no change: the value it re-inserts
+  comes from removing it from the working clone, so it is already a uniquely-held reference with no
+  other alias to protect against.
+- Added failing-first regression tests to `src/index.test.ts` for both bugs (a member literally named
+  `__proto__` via `add` and `replace`; `add`'s and `replace`'s value no longer aliased after the call,
+  including two `add` operations sharing one literal) before applying each fix.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test`: 43/43 passing (was 38/38 before this task; 5 new tests added, all were red before their
+  matching fix and green after).
+- `npm run typecheck`: no errors.
